{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed56627177/13/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6e453dd6\" lines=\"39-52\">\n<description>\nSlide black shapes rightward to a grey separator, then shoot red rays from enclosed holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- A vertical grey (5) separator column divides the grid into left and right halves.\n- Black shapes sit on the left side against a background color (bg).\n\nRule:\n- Translate each black connected component rightward until its right edge is flush with the separator.\n- After sliding, find enclosed bg regions (holes) within the black shapes that touch the column just left of the separator.\n- For each row spanned by such a hole, emit a red ray across the entire right half of the grid.\n\nKey insight: Only holes adjacent to the separator trigger rays — the sliding creates enclosures that act as \"nozzles\" projecting rightward.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5b37cb25\" lines=\"59-73\">\n<description>\nFill each plus-shaped background notch with the color of the frame side it opens toward.\n\nInput structure\n- A one-cell-thick rectangular frame has four solid-colored sides and neutral corner pixels.\n- The frame interior contains a dominant background color and one foreground object color.\n- Some object boundaries contain five-pixel plus-shaped background notches.\n\nRule\n- Find every background plus whose opposite side is supported by foreground object pixels and whose open side faces one frame side.\n- Recolor the five pixels of that plus with the color of the facing frame side.\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\nThe frame side colors are direction labels for the open notches in the foreground object.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9bbf930d\" lines=\"61-72\">\n<description>\nMove magenta particles along corridors formed by parallel colored bars.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg=7. Particles=6 in col 0. Colored bars on even rows form corridors on odd rows.\n\nRule:\n- Particle goes straight through bg cells.\n- When blocked by a colored cell, turns 90° into the perpendicular direction\n  that has a bg cell flanked by same-color bars. Stops at grid edge or full blockage.\n\nKey insight: Same-color parallel bars define the only valid corridors, so each particle follows a deterministic local trace.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1a244afd\" lines=\"49-68\">\n<description>\nRotate each magenta source pixel 90 degrees counterclockwise around its matched blue pivot, recoloring the new pixel orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The only foreground colors are blue pivot pixels and magenta source pixels.\n- Each magenta source can be paired one-to-one with a blue pivot in the same\n  row or column.\n\nRule:\n- Remove all magenta source pixels.\n- For each matched source/pivot pair, preserve the source distance from the\n  pivot but rotate its offset 90 degrees counterclockwise.\n- Paint the rotated in-bounds target orange; if the target is outside the\n  grid, the source simply disappears.\n\nKey insight:\nThe blue pixels are rotation centers, and row/column alignment gives a\nunique global matching from each magenta source to its pivot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"c7f57c3e\" lines=\"22-35\">\n<description>\nSwap every sprite to the opposite prototype while preserving scale and position.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Two distinct small sprite patterns (prototypes) appear at various scales.\n- Each sprite instance is a scaled copy of one of the two prototypes, placed on the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the two prototype patterns from the smallest-area sprites.\n- For each sprite, determine which prototype it matches and its integer scale factor.\n- Replace it with the other prototype at the same scale, aligned by an internal anchor region (color-1 pixels).\n\nKey insight: Every sprite toggles to the alternate prototype; the anchor sub-region inside each sprite pins the alignment so swapped sprites land in the correct position regardless of scale.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e12f9a14\" lines=\"138-152\">\n<description>\nEmit diagonal beams from framed 2x2 blocks through frame gaps; beams interact on collision.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall color (e.g. green) forms 4x4 frames around 2x2 monochrome blocks.\n- Each frame has one or more gap pixels where the wall is missing.\n\nRule:\n- Each gap emits a beam of the inner block's color, traveling diagonally outward (direction set by gap position relative to the block).\n- Beams paint every cell they cross until they hit the grid edge or collide.\n- 90° collision: both beams turn 45° toward their bisector direction; the intersection cell is left blank (1-pixel gap).\n- 45° collision: beams merge into a zigzag (alternating cardinal + diagonal steps) with no gap at the meeting point.\n\nKey insight: Two distinct collision rules — 90° produces a gap and single-pixel diagonal lines; 45° produces no gap and thicker zigzag bands.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1efba499\" lines=\"70-85\">\n<description>\nSwap paired marker pixels across the largest foreground-color barrier, placing them just outside the barrier segment.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One foreground color is the unique most frequent non-background color; its pixels form the stationary barrier.\n- Other foreground pixels are single markers, sometimes paired on opposite sides of one contiguous barrier segment in the same row or column.\n\nRule:\n- Scan each row and column that crosses exactly one contiguous barrier segment.\n- If the line has exactly one marker on each side of that segment, erase both source markers and place each marker in the cell immediately beyond the opposite side of the segment.\n- Lines with markers on only one side are unchanged; the barrier remains unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marker pairing is local to a row or column, and the destination is defined by the nearest cells outside the intervening barrier segment, not by the original distance from the barrier.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a25697e4\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nDock movable tools into border-anchored frames by filling cavities.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Border-touching components cluster into anchor frames with interior bg cavities.\n- Non-border 2-color components are movable tools.\n\nRule:\n- Match each tool to a frame whose cavity size equals one of its color-region counts.\n- Rotate/flip the tool so one color fills the cavity exactly; the other stays outside.\n- If the docking orientation uses a reflection, swap the tool's two colors.\n\nKey insight: Cavity size determines tool–frame pairing; reflection triggers color swap.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"963c33f8\" lines=\"86-108\">\n<description>\nDrop the columns of the top marker downward, stopping blue-ended columns on grey obstacles and letting maroon-ended columns pass to the bottom.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The most numerous non-background color forms stationary grey obstacle pixels.\n- The other two colors form a single 3x3 top-edge marker whose top two rows\n  are maroon and whose bottom row contains maroon or blue control pixels.\n\nRule\n- Erase the top marker.\n- Treat each marker column as a vertical drop retaining its three colors.\n- A column whose bottom control pixel is blue stops immediately above the\n  first grey pixel below that column, or bottom-aligns if none exists.\n- Contiguous maroon-ended columns pass through obstacles and bottom-align;\n  if such a run has a clear down-right slide from a grey ledge, shift the\n  run one column right before bottom-aligning.\n- When a maroon-ended drop lands through a grey bar on the bottom edge, clear\n  the rest of the marker-width gap in that bottom bar.\n\nKey insight\n- The bottom row of the top marker is a control strip: blue cells mean\n  \"stick to the first obstacle,\" while maroon cells mean \"fall through.\"\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"31f7f899\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nSort vertical bars by height and redistribute them across fixed colored slots.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; a horizontal base line of a single color spans the full grid width.\n- Several monochrome vertical bars of distinct colors rise upward from the base line.\n\nRule:\n- Rank bar shapes by height (shortest to tallest).\n- Rank original x-positions (slots) left to right.\n- Place the i-th shortest shape into the i-th leftmost slot, recoloring it to that slot's original color.\n- The base line is preserved unchanged.\n\nKey insight: Each slot keeps its x-position and color identity; only the bar silhouettes are redistributed among slots by height order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"afe3afe9\" lines=\"106-119\">\n<description>\nConvert each hollow 3x3 symbol to one pixel and slide the pixels clockwise from the marked edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a background, one full-edge marker line, and monochrome hollow 3x3 foreground symbols.\n- The symbols lie on a coarse row/column lattice; fully empty lattice rows or columns are absent from the output.\n\nRule\n- Replace each hollow 3x3 symbol with one pixel of its color in the corresponding coarse-grid cell.\n- The marker edge gives the compaction direction by clockwise border travel: top -> right, right -> down, bottom -> left, left -> up.\n- Preserve the order of non-background pixels while sliding them as far as possible in that direction.\n\nKey insight\n- The large picture is a scaled symbolic grid, and the blue edge marker acts as a gravity direction rather than as output content.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c61be7dc\" lines=\"42-57\">\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
